854:
A. It shall be prohibited in all residential districts to park or store in the open, wrecked or junked vehicles, power driven construction equipment, or any miscellaneous scrap or salvageable material in quantity, e.g., used lumber, metal, rubbish, or appliances.
1. No automobile or vehicle of any kind or type, without a valid license plate attached thereto, shall be parked or stand on any residentially-zoned property unless it shall be in a completely enclosed building except for such off-road vehicles which by law do not require a license plate, provided the same is in operating condition.
2. No automobile or vehicle of any kind or type, which shall be inoperative or in a junk condition, or abandoned, shall be parked or stand on any property unless:
a. It shall be in an enclosed building;
b. A vehicle in inoperative or junk condition shall include, but shall not be limited to, any automobile, vehicle, trailer of any kind or type, or contrivance, or a part thereof, the condition of which is one or more of the following:
1) Wrecked;
2) Dismantled;
3) Partially dismantled;
4) Inoperative;
5) Abandoned;
6) Discarded;
7) Scrapped; or
8) Does not have a valid license plate attached thereto.
c. Nothing in this section shall authorize the maintenance of a public or private nuisance as defined under other provisions of law.
d. This section shall not be the exclusive regulation of abandoned, discarded, dismantled, wrecked, scrapped, ruined or junk motor vehicles, or contrivances, within the unincorporated limits of the county, but shall be supplemental to and in addition to other regulations and ordinances of the county and statutes or provisions of law heretofore and hereinafter enacted by the county, state or other legal entity or agency having jurisdiction.
e. For purposes of repair or restoration, one inoperable and unregistered vehicle may be kept in the rear yard of a property provided it is not visible from the street, is screened from all neighbors by a privacy fence in the rear yard, and is repaired or restored, removed, or placed in a fully enclosed building within six months. Such vehicle shall not be stored in any side yard or any setback or buffer area.
B. Commercial vehicles and trailers exceeding 18 feet in length shall not be placed or stored in R-1, R-2, R-3, R-1L or RR districts.
1. For the purposes of this section, commercial vehicles and trailers shall include commercial motor vehicles, as defined in section 34-76 of this Code of Ordinances, tractor trailers, boat trailers, campers, buses, tractors
2. This prohibition shall not apply to residential parcels of two or more acres of land. Commercial vehicles, tractors, and trailers may be parked or stored, as those terms are defined in section 34-76 of the Code of Ordinances, on a residential parcel of two or more acres of land, however, commercial vehicles must be parked or stored, beyond the front yard building line of the residence. Additionally, only one tractor or commercial vehicle may be parked on stored on a residential lot.
3. Commercial vehicles and trailers shall not be parked on vacant lots in residential districts, irrespective of the size of the lot.
4. Notwithstanding any restrictions of this appendix, to the contrary, recreational vehicles and trailers less than 18 feet in length may be parked on residential properties, regardless of lot size, however the trailer or recreational vehicle must be parked or stored beyond the front yard building line of the residence.
5. The restrictions of this section on the location for storing or parking trailers less than 18 feet and recreational vehicles shall not apply in the R-1L district.
(Ord. of 7-16-24(2), § 4)

857:
Any new single -family residential dwelling built or otherwise placed in any residential district in Sumter County shall:
A. Be required to be 1,500 square feet. placed on a permanent foundation;
B. Be connected to an approved sewage disposal system, potable water service or well, and electrical service;
C. Contain a water heater in safe and working condition;
D. Have all switches, receptacles, fixtures, etc. properly installed, wired and in working condition. Distribution panels shall be in compliance with the approved listing, complete with required breakers, with all unused openings covered with solid covers and listed for that purpose. The home shall be subject to an electrical continuity test to assure that all metallic parts are properly bonded;
E. Contain at least one bathroom with a sanitary, connected and working toilet and tub and/or shower;
F. Contain at least one operational window of sufficient size to allow egress in case of an emergency;
G. Contain at least one operate battery-powered smoke detector in each bedroom and in the kitchen, which must be installed in accordance with the manufacturer's recommendations; and
H. Meet all applicable county building, electrical and plumbing codes, and current ICC codes.
(Ord. of 7-16-24(2), § 4)

859:
The R-1 district is composed of certain areas having a single-family residential character and undeveloped land where it is desirable and likely that similar development will occur. This district will be characterized by lot sizes designed for medium density residential development. The specific purpose of this district is to:
A. Encourage the construction and continued use of land for single-family dwellings constructed on site;
B. Prohibit business, commercial or industrial use of the land, and prohibit any other use which would substantially interfere with development or maintenance of single-family dwellings in the district;
C. Encourage the discontinuance of existing uses that would not be permitted as new uses under the provisions of this appendix;
D. Discourage any land use which would generate traffic on minor or local roads, other than normal traffic to serve residences on those roads;
E. Discourage any use which, because of its size or character, would create requirements and costs for public services such as fire and police protection, water supply and sanitary sewerage substantially in excess of such requirements and costs if the district were developed solely for residential purposes.
(Ord. of 7-16-24(2), § 6)

